# Acceptance harness ledger (.aisoc/acceptance-history.jsonl is per-machine and
# accumulates across runs — useful for "is this getting slower?" but not for
# version control. Same applies to any other harness state we drop in here.)
.aisoc/

# Detection-import upstream clones (populated by tools.detection_import)
.import-cache/

# Docusaurus generated cache (apps/docs)
apps/docs/.docusaurus/
apps/docs/build/

# Local QA artifacts (screenshots from manual UI checks)
apps/web/.qa-screenshots/

# Runtime playbook store. `PlaybookStore` keeps the bundled corpus read-only and
# writes every mutation to `index.json` in the same directory, so anything that
# exercises `POST /api/v1/playbooks` — a test, a local run, a reproduction of a
# bug — leaves a several-thousand-line file behind. Committing it ships whatever
# the last run happened to hold as if it were curated content, and the playbook
# schema lint counts it as a 65th file.
services/agents/data/playbooks/index.json

# Marketplace index staged into the API service build context at deploy time
# (see infra/fly/fly-demo-deploy.sh). The canonical source is marketplace/index.json
# at the repo root; the API Dockerfile only sees its own dir as build context, so
# the deploy script copies the index in just-in-time. We never commit the copy.
services/api/marketplace/

# Live-dumped graph schema produced by `scripts/export_graph_schema.py`
# (default mode). The source of truth is `schemas/graph-schema.yaml`; the
# `-current.yaml` file is a runtime artefact that varies by environment.
schemas/graph-schema-current.yaml


# Compiled Go binaries. `services/demo-producer/demo-producer` was committed as
# a 6.9 MB macOS arm64 executable in a repository whose Dockerfile builds a
# GOOS=linux one — nothing consumed it, nothing rebuilt it, and running the
# documented `go build ./...` silently overwrote it and dirtied the tree.
/services/demo-producer/demo-producer
/services/enrichment/enrichment
/services/ingest/ingest

# Generated by scripts/resolve_port_conflicts.py when a host port AiSOC
# publishes is already in use. Machine-specific by definition.
docker-compose.ports.yml

# Compiled Go test binaries. `go test -c` drops one beside the source named
# after its directory, and one was committed in May 2026: 4.2 MB of Mach-O
# arm64 that nothing referenced, carrying the absolute build paths of the
# machine that produced it. A binary cannot be reviewed, is wrong for every
# architecture but one, and embeds whatever directory its author happened to
# work in.
plugins/*/go/*-test
*.test

# Pre-upgrade database dumps written by `make upgrade`. A dump holds every
# `vault:v1:` connector credential, every alert and every case in the
# deployment, so committing one would publish the lot — and the directory
# only exists on a host that has actually been upgraded, which is never a
# contributor's checkout.
/backups/
